  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon there is an upper-level ridge over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring slightly cooler\ntemperatures to the area with dry weather. On Monday and Tuesday \nthe upper-level ridge remains over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This will will keep warm and dry weather for\nthe area on Monday and Tuesday. Smoke from regional wildfires \nwill be around throughout the next few days. On Wednesday the \nupper-level ridge remains over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana with an upper-level trough approaching the \narea. This will begin to bring cooler air and monsoonal moisture \nto the area on Wednesday.\n\nThursday through Friday an upper-level trough moves over North- \ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring \nprecipitation chances and cooler temperatures (well-below seasonal\naverages) to the area Thursday through Friday. See the Forecast \nConfidence & Scenarios section for details. Next weekend an upper-\nlevel ridge builds back in over North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana with southwest flow aloft. This will bring \ndaily chances for showers and thunderstorms with temperatures \nwarming up on Saturday and next Sunday. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nToday and tomorrow across North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana minimum afternoon humidity will be near \ncritical levels. This combined wind gusts that will peak at around\n20 mph will bring elevated fire weather conditions to the area. \nThe wind direction will also periodically shift over the next 24 \nto 48 hours. The greatest risk for elevated to near critical fire \nweather conditions is in Southwestern Montana this afternoon \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nWednesday through Friday there are differences in the track of \nthe upper-level trough in ensembles and deterministic models. On \nThursday and Friday one cluster has zonal flow over Montana while \nthe other three have an upper-level trough approaching Montana. In\nthe zonal flow scenario temperatures will remain at about \nseasonal averages with a few isolated showers. In the upper-level\ntrough scenario temperatures will cool off to at least below \nseasonal averages with more widespread showers. Another area of \nuncertainty with this trough is how far south it tracks. The \nfurther south it tracks, the further south the precipitation will \nbe and it will result in cooler temperatures than a more northerly\ntrack.\n\nOn Wednesday there is a 30 - 45% chance for thunderstorms across \nthe Hi-Line and a 20 - 30% chance for thunderstorms across the \nrest of North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. On \nThursday there is a 20 - 40% chance for thunderstorms across most \nof North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. On Friday \nthere is a 35% chance for thunderstorms across Central and \nSouthwestern Montana. Wednesday through Friday there is a 40 - 80%\nchance for a quarter inch of rain or greater across North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana with the highest \nprobabilities along the Hi-Line. Wednesday through Friday there is\na 50 - 70% chance for one half inch or rain or greater along the \nHi-Line and across Choteau, Judith Basin, and Fergus Counties. On \nThursday and Friday there is greater than a 50% chance for high \ntemperatures of 70 degrees or lower across the plains of North-\ncentral Montana. -IG",
